1. e4 e6 {Before the Hastings Tournament, I had written off to a friend in
Finland to obtain the scores of all the games played by Sterner when he won
the Scandinavian title in Helsinki this summer. He had sent back a packet of
French and Slav Defences, and hence I had spent most of the morning before the
game preparing for this move. At lunch I casually mentioned this to Peter
Clarke, who asked me whether I had looked at the line of play which arises
from Black’s 6th and 9th moves in the present game. I had to admit that I
hadn’t and so, generously fore­going my turn to pay for after-lunch coffee,
I went up to my room, got out my copy of Keres book on the French, and did my
neglected homework.} 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Qc7 7.
Qg4 Ne7 ({A line of play inferior to Botvinnik’s move} 7... f5) 8. Qxg7 Rg8
9. Qxh7 cxd4 10. Ne2 (10. Ra2 {and 10 Kd1 have been tried here, but the text
is sounder.}) 10... Nbc6 11. f4 ({Not} 11. cxd4 Nxd4 $1) 11... Bd7 12. Qd3 dxc3
13. Be3 ({The obvious} 13. Nxc3 {leaves White with some troubles on the c-file
after} a6 {followed by ...Rc8 and ...Na5. What White in fact has in mind from
now on is an ending in which Black will be left with a bishop obstructed by
its own pawns, and White with a much more active piece, be it the f1-bishop or
the knight. Of course, such an ending is also likely to favour White because
of the passed h-pawn. Black's counter-chances lie in his central pawn majority,
and hence in the possibility of one of the thrusts . ...d4 or ...f6. White
prevents the first as soon as possible by firmly occupying d4, so that the
strategy of the whole game revolves round whether Black can open the centre
before his own king gets into trouble on the queenside.}) 13... Nf5 14. Nd4
Nfxd4 15. Bxd4 O-O-O 16. g3 {Freeing the f1-bishop.} Kb8 ({Black plans some
continuation like} 16... Rdf8 17. Bxc3 d4 18. Bxd4 Nxd4 19. Qxd4 Qxc2 {, which
would at the moment be too dangerous because of the unprotected a-pawn and the
open c-file.}) 17. Be2 Nxd4 ({And here the immediate} 17... Rdf8 {does not
work owing to} 18. Bc5 Nxe5 19. Qxc3 Ng6 20. Bxa7+) 18. Qxd4 Rc8 $2 ({But this
loss of time is difficult to understand, except on the grounds that Black
toyed with the idea of changing his plan and swapping queens by ...Qc5—which
White’s reply at once discourages. The immediate} 18... Rdf8 {is correct.})
19. Kf2 $1 Rg6 ({If} 19... Qc5 20. Ke3 Qxd4+ 21. Kxd4 {, with a won ending for
White, since the c-pawn must soon fall.}) 20. Bd3 Rh6 21. Rab1 Rf8 22. Rb3 f6
23. Ba6 $1 b6 ({23 Ba6 was just in time. Black cannot now play} 23... fxe5 24.
Rxb7+ Qxb7 25. Qxe5+ Qc7 26. Rb1+ {and wins}) (23... Bc8 24. Bxb7 $1 Bxb7 25.
Rhb1 Rxh2+ 26. Kf3 Rfh8 ({if} 26... Rxc2 27. Rxb7+ Qxb7 28. Rxb7+ Kxb7 29. Qb4+
) 27. Rxb7+ Qxb7 28. Rxb7+ Kxb7 29. exf6 {and the passed f-pawn wins easily.})
24. Rxc3 Qd8 ({I had a moment of terror over the possible reply} 24... Qxe5 {
but then} 25. Qb4 $1 {wins.}) 25. Qb4 {Now there is no salvation for Black.}
Ka8 26. Qd6 fxe5 27. Rc7 Rxf4+ 28. Kg2 $1 ({But not} 28. gxf4 $2 Qh4+ {, with
good chances of perpetual check.}) 28... Qf8 29. Bb7+ ({The crude way; more
elegant was} 29. Qxd7 Rf2+ 30. Kg1 {when Black is defenceless.}) 29... Kb8 30.
Rc8+ {Sources: BCM, February 1958, p52; The Times, 8 January 1958} 1-0

