{NO MELANCHOLY IN THIS DANE - Few games, I imagine, cause more headaches in
Fleet Street than chess. On top of the seemingly unsurmountable difficulty of
getting the diagrams printed the right way up, there is the omnipresent bother
with the names of the foreign competitors. After all, just con­sider:
Dunkelblum, Iljin-Genevsky, Kieseritzky, Dus-Chotimirsky, Gligoric. At the
last Congress I was glad to welcome a far-from-melancholy Dane, Mr. Malte
Lemvig-Fogg. It was his first visit to Hastings: I am pleased to say that he
gave the town and its inhabitants a high mark. Here is a smart game which he
played in the Premier Reserves Major. The annotations are from 'Hamlet, Prince
of Denmark.'} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 {Springes to catch woodcock.} cxd4 {
Have at you now!} 4. c3 dxc3 5. Nxc3 e6 6. Bc4 a6 7. O-O b5 8. Bb3 Nf6 9. e5
Ng4 10. Bf4 Be7 11. Ne4 g5 {O day and night, but this is wondrous strange!} 12.
Nexg5 Ngxe5 13. Nxe5 Bxg5 {Angels and ministers of grace defend us!} 14. Qh5 {
Look to the Queen there, ho!} Nxe5 15. Bxg5 {For 'tis the sport to have the
engineer hoist with his own petard.} Qc7 16. Bf6 Rg8 17. Bxe5 Bb7 {It is the
poison'd cup! It is too late.} 18. Bg3 {Ho! let the door be locked.} Qc6 {
Diseases desperate grown. By desperate appliances are reliev'd. Or not at all.}
19. f3 Qb6+ 20. Kh1 Qd4 21. Rfe1 Qxb2 22. Bxe6 {They say he made a good end.}
dxe6 23. Rxe6+ {He was a man, take him for all in all. I shall not look upon
his like again. (Source: Hastings and St Leonards Observer, 16 August 1958)}
1-0

