{"So the Premier was quiet. There were, however, no fewer than sixteen other
tournaments at Hastings, and in one of these was played quite the most
fantastic game I have seen for many a day. To drive your opponent’s king out
into the open board is the average chess-player’s most cherished dream. In
perhaps one game out of a hundred you manage to bring this off: and you have
your opponent at your mercy.  Here, Black’s king is driven from his original
square right down to c2, but on arriving there, instead of meeting a grisly
doom, he actively participates in subjecting his opposite number to a snap
mate-in-three. You must excuse me from annotating the game : the very thought
of it leaves me speechless." (BH Wood)} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. Ng5
Bc5 5. Nxf7 Bxf2+ 6. Kf1 Qe7 7. Nxh8 d5 8. Nc3 $5 $146 (8. exd5) 8... Bb6 $6 (
8... dxc4 9. Kxf2 Bg4 10. Qf1 O-O-O $44) 9. Nxd5 Nxd5 10. Bxd5 Nd4 11. d3 $2 (
11. Bf7+ $18) 11... Qf6+ 12. Ke1 Qh4+ 13. g3 Qh3 14. Bf7+ Ke7 15. Bg5+ Kd6 16.
Bh5 Qg2 17. Nf7+ Kc5 18. b4+ Kc6 19. Nxe5+ Kd6 20. Nc4+ Kc6 21. Be8+ Bd7 22.
b5+ (22. Bxd7+ Kxd7 23. Qg4+ Ke8 24. Rf1 $3 $18 Nxc2+ $140 25. Kd1 Nd4 26. Nd2)
22... Nxb5 23. Bxd7+ Kxd7 24. Qg4+ Kc6 25. Ne5+ (25. Qe6+ Nd6 26. Qd5+ Kd7 27.
Ne5+ Kc8 28. Qg8+ Ne8 29. Qxe8#) 25... Kc5 26. Be7+ (26. Be3+ Nd4 27. Bxd4+
Kxd4 28. Nf3+ Kc3 29. Qxg7+ Kxc2 30. Rc1+ $3 $18) 26... Kd4 27. Qd7+ $4 (27.
Bb4 $3 $18) 27... Kc3 28. Bb4+ Kxc2 $4 (28... Kxb4 $15) 29. Rf1 Rf8 30. Rxf8 $4
(30. Nf7 $18) 30... Qg1+ 31. Rf1 $4 (31. Ke2 $5 Nd4+ 32. Qxd4 $8 Qxd4 33. Rh1
$1 Qxb4 $17) 31... Qe3# {Source: Illustrated London News, 25 January 1958 
(Ellison and Hall shared 1st place on 5½/9 - round played not known so date
could be ??.01.1958)} 0-1

